 r←HouseKeepingIfOverdue server;now;interval
⍝ Runs house keeping when a full interval has gone by since it last ran, even though Conga
⍝ has reported an event rather than a timeout. Returns 1 if it ran and 0 if it did not.
⍝ Without this, `ServerWait` reaches house keeping only when `DRC.Wait` returns 100, and
⍝ Conga returns that only when nothing at all arrived for the whole of `CongaTimeout`.
⍝ Every event starts that window again, so a server asked for something every few seconds
⍝ would never run house keeping at all: idle connections would not be reaped, upstream
⍝ health would not be checked, and whatever the application hung off `OnCongaTimeout` -
⍝ log rotation, say, or noticing that it has been asked to shut down - would not happen
⍝ either. The interval is meant to be "every so often", not "once things go quiet".
⍝ House keeping is run before the event that prompted it is handled, so the work it does
⍝ lands in front of one request rather than being put off indefinitely. It is expected to
⍝ be brief; anything lengthy belongs in a thread of the application's own.
⍝ `LastHouseKeeping` is 0 on a Server space that has not house kept yet (see `NewServer`).
⍝ That first sighting only starts the clock: a server is given a full interval before its
⍝ first house keeping, which is what would happen were it idle.
⍝ Should the clock be put back - at the end of daylight saving time, say - the clock is simply
⍝ started again; otherwise house keeping would be suspended until the time had caught up.
 r←0
 now←SecondsNow
 interval←0.001×server.CongaTimeout                  ⍝ Conga counts milliseconds, SecondsNow seconds
 :If 0=server.LastHouseKeeping
 :OrIf now<server.LastHouseKeeping
     server.LastHouseKeeping←now
 :ElseIf interval≤now-server.LastHouseKeeping
     {}RunHouseKeeping server
     r←1
 :EndIf
⍝Done
